in kubernetes world the imperative way of managing infrastructure is using commands like

kubectl run --image=nginx nginx
#this will create a pod

kubectl create deployment --image=nginx nginx
#this will create a deployment

kubectl expose deployment nginx --port 80
#this will create a service to expose exisiting deployment

kubectl edit deployment nginix
#this used to edit nginx deployment
#kubectl edit -> used to edit exisiting object

kubectl scale deployment nginx replicas=5
#for scaling replicas in deployment

kubectl set image deployment nginix nginix=nginix.1.10
#updating the image in pods in exisiting deployment, using kubectl set image

#we also use object configuration file to create and manage objects

#creating an object using configuration file
kubectl create -f nginx.yaml

#editing an object created via configuration file
kubectl replace -f nginix.yaml

#deleting the objects and resources created via configuration file
kubectl delete -f nginix.yaml

all of these are imperative approach in creating and managing objects in kubernetes